title: SvelteKit Fullstack Walkthrough
slug: sveltekit
heading: SvelteKit Fullstack Walkthrough
createdAt: 2024-09-04T00:00:00.000Z
updatedAt: 2024-09-04T00:00:00.000Z
---

## Overview

This guide shows how to connect your SvelteKit frontend sessions with server-side errors, logs, and traces using Highlight. The usual flow is:

1. Initialize the client SDK in `hooks.client.ts` so the browser records sessions and sends the `x-highlight-request` header on network calls.
2. Initialize the Node backend SDK in `hooks.server.ts` or your server entry-point and use `H.runWithHeaders` / `H.startWithHeaders` to propagate trace/session context and record server-side errors and spans.

See our Fullstack Mapping doc for more context: /docs/getting-started/2_frontend-backend-mapping

## Installation

Install the client and server packages in your SvelteKit project:

```bash
# client (frontend)
yarn add highlight.run

# server (Node backend)
yarn add @highlight-run/node
```

(Use `npm` or `pnpm` if you prefer.)

## Client (quick reminder)

Instrument the browser bundle in `src/hooks.client.ts` (or your app entry point) using the `highlight.run` package. Make sure to enable `tracingOrigins` and `networkRecording.recordHeadersAndBody` so the client includes the `x-highlight-request` header on outgoing requests.

Example (hooks.client.ts):

```ts
// src/hooks.client.ts
import { H } from 'highlight.run';

H.init('<YOUR_PROJECT_ID>', {
  serviceName: 'my-sveltekit-frontend',
  tracingOrigins: true, // or a list of origins that include your backend
  networkRecording: {
    enabled: true,
    recordHeadersAndBody: true,
  },
});
```

See the browser SvelteKit quickstart for more details: /docs/getting-started/browser/sveltekit

## Server instrumentation (Node)

This section assumes you are running SvelteKit on a Node-compatible runtime (for example, using `@sveltejs/adapter-node` or any environment that provides a Node process). The `@highlight-run/node` SDK requires a Node environment; it is not compatible with Edge runtimes like Cloudflare Workers or Vercel Edge Functions.

A common place to initialize the server SDK and create a top-level span is `src/hooks.server.ts`. The examples below show how to:

- Initialize `H` once.
- Use `H.runWithHeaders` to wrap request handling so trace/session context from the client is attached to server spans.
- Parse request headers and call `H.consumeError` so errors are linked to the originating frontend session.

### Example: src/hooks.server.ts

```ts
// src/hooks.server.ts
import type { Handle } from '@sveltejs/kit';
import { H } from '@highlight-run/node';

// Initialize once during module import. Use env vars for projectID/serviceVersion.
if (!H.isInitialized()) {
  H.init({
    projectID: process.env.HIGHLIGHT_PROJECT_ID ?? '<YOUR_PROJECT_ID>',
    serviceName: 'sveltekit-backend',
    serviceVersion: process.env.COMMIT_SHA,
    // debug: true // enable for verbose server-side logging
  });
}

export const handle: Handle = async ({ event, resolve }) => {
  // Convert the Fetch Headers to a plain object carrier. This avoids carrier compatibility
  // issues and ensures header parsing/propagation works reliably.
  const headers = Object.fromEntries(event.request.headers);

  return await H.runWithHeaders('sveltekit.handle', headers, async (span) => {
    try {
      // You can set attributes on the active span if useful
      span.setAttribute('http.route', event.url.pathname);

      const response = await resolve(event);

      return response;
    } catch (err) {
      // Parse any Highlight session/request ids and attach to the error we send
      const parsed = H.parseHeaders(headers);

      if (err instanceof Error) {
        H.consumeError(err, parsed?.secureSessionId, parsed?.requestId);
      } else {
        H.consumeError(new Error(JSON.stringify(err)), parsed?.secureSessionId, parsed?.requestId);
      }

      // re-throw so SvelteKit can continue handling as normal
      throw err;
    }
  });
};
```

Notes:
- We convert the `Headers` instance to a plain object via `Object.fromEntries(event.request.headers)`. Passing a plain object avoids incompatibilities with certain propagation/injection code paths.
- `H.runWithHeaders` will start a span, inject the trace context into the provided headers carrier, and ensure any child spans created with the Node SDK are tied to the same trace.
- Use `H.parseHeaders` to extract `secureSessionId` and `requestId` values if you need to attach them to errors or logs.

### Example: instrumenting an API route (+server.ts)

```ts
// src/routes/api/hello/+server.ts
import { H } from '@highlight-run/node';
import type { RequestHandler } from './$types';

export const GET: RequestHandler = async ({ request }) => {
  const headers = Object.fromEntries(request.headers);

  return await H.runWithHeaders('api.hello', headers, async (span) => {
    try {
      // do some work
      return new Response(JSON.stringify({ message: 'ok' }), { status: 200, headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' } });
    } catch (err) {
      const parsed = H.parseHeaders(headers);
      H.consumeError(err instanceof Error ? err : new Error(String(err)), parsed?.secureSessionId, parsed?.requestId);
      throw err;
    }
  });
};
```

### Example: server-side load functions (+page.server.ts)

```ts
// src/routes/dashboard/+page.server.ts
import { H } from '@highlight-run/node';
import type { PageServerLoad } from './$types';

export const load: PageServerLoad = async ({ request }) => {
  const headers = Object.fromEntries(request.headers);

  return await H.runWithHeaders('page.dashboard.load', headers, async (span) => {
    // Fetch data, run DB queries, etc. You can also set attributes on the active span.
    span.setAttribute('page', 'dashboard');
    const data = await fetchSomeData();
    return { data };
  });
};
```

## Forwarding highlight context to other backend services

If your backend makes outbound requests to other services (microservices, third-party APIs), forward the incoming `x-highlight-request` header on outgoing requests so downstream services can continue the mapping between frontend sessions and backend traces/errors.

Example when using fetch:

```ts
const incoming = Object.fromEntries(request.headers);
const forwarded = { 'x-highlight-request': incoming['x-highlight-request'] };
await fetch('https://other-service.example/api', { headers: forwarded });
```

## CORS and headers

If your frontend and backend are on different origins, ensure your backend's CORS configuration allows the `x-highlight-request` header in `Access-Control-Allow-Headers`, otherwise the browser will not be able to send the header in cross-origin requests.

## Edge runtimes

The examples above use `@highlight-run/node` which requires a Node environment. If you are deploying SvelteKit to an Edge runtime (Cloudflare Workers, Vercel Edge Functions, etc.), use the appropriate Highlight SDK for that runtime (for example, the Cloudflare/Edge SDKs) or use OpenTelemetry-compatible tools. See the SDK docs for supported runtimes: /docs/sdk/nodejs

## How to verify it works

1. Ensure the browser SDK is initialized with `tracingOrigins` and `networkRecording.recordHeadersAndBody: true`.
2. Enable `debug: true` in your server `H.init` during testing to see SDK logs in the server console.
3. Trigger a server-side error (throw inside an endpoint or load function). Look for the error in the Highlight dashboard and confirm the error is linked to a session.
4. In your browser devtools, inspect network requests and confirm a `x-highlight-request` header is being sent to your backend.
5. In the Highlight Traces view, look for spans created by the server that include the `highlight.session_id` attribute.

## Common pitfalls

- Not converting `Headers` to a plain object when calling `H.runWithHeaders` / `H.parseHeaders` — prefer `Object.fromEntries(event.request.headers)`.
- Running SvelteKit in an Edge runtime while using the Node SDK — pick the SDK appropriate for your runtime.
- Forgetting to enable `tracingOrigins` and `networkRecording` on the client.
- Not allowing `x-highlight-request` in CORS `Access-Control-Allow-Headers` for cross-origin setups.

